{"uncertainty": 0.22, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "Root cause of the uart_tx regression failure: data register reloaded while a frame is in flight", "summary": "Conclusion: rtl_bug, from reading [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1]. DEFECT: inside the non-reset branch, before the state case, the RTL has `if (start) hold <= data;`. This runs in every state, so the shift data register is overwritten whenever start is high. INCORRECTNESS: the header of the same file specifies 8N1 framing and says 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The reload contradicts that clause. Reloading belongs only in the IDLE branch, which already contains `hold <= data`. CAUSALITY: a start pulse during START, DATA or STOP writes new data into hold. Every later data bit is then driven from `tx <= hold[bit_idx + 3'd1]`. The fault is therefore written at the cycle start is sampled mid-frame, but it only becomes visible on the line one bit later, at the next bit boundary. It then propagates as wrong data bits for the rest of the frame. The checker sees it only when the received byte is compared. This fits the triage observation: one isolated failed check of 4, with a normal $finish ([evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2], [run:run-0002]). VeriTriage's weak top hypothesis 'Likely RTL bug' (unknown_failure, 30% confidence) was treated as one candidate, not as proof ([evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e1], [run:run-0001]). RANKED HYPOTHESES AND WHAT REFUTES EACH: (1) RTL bug, accepted. Supported by the reload line contradicting the header clause in [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1]. It would be refuted if the new repro, whose expected values come from the spec, passed on this RTL. It would also be refuted if the same repro failed on the clean RTL. (2) Testbench bug, ranked second, not ruled out by experiment. I cannot see the upstream triage testbench's expected values. My repro derives every expected value from the 8N1 clause and the 'ignored while in flight' clause, and copies nothing from the RTL's output. It would be refuted if it failed on the clean RTL. (3) Infrastructure, rejected on current evidence. The failing run built, reached $finish at 546000, and counted 1 of 4 failed checks ([evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2]). That pattern does not fit a build or tool fault. It would be revived if a clean rebuild with no other change passed. (4) Spec ambiguity, rejected. The clause 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored' fixes the behaviour explicitly. It would be revived if the governing specification, which is not visible to me beyond the RTL header, said otherwise. REPRO: uart_tx_rootcause_repro.v (top uart_tx_rootcause_repro_tb). It sends 0xA5, pulses start with 0x3C in the middle of data bit 2, and expects 0xA5 to be received. On this RTL, bits 3..7 come from 0x3C, so the expected received byte is 0x3D, and exactly 1 of 5 checks should fail. On RTL that reloads hold only in IDLE, all 5 should pass. That expected outcome is a prediction; no run of this file is cited. Traces to [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. Suggested fix, not made here: delete the unconditional reload, and add an assertion that hold is stable while busy."}], "files": [{"path": "uart_tx_rootcause_repro.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: start pulse during a frame must not change the transmitted byte", "summary": "Repro for [requirement:req-d10c4df532] against [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1]. It receives frames by detecting the start-bit falling edge and sampling each bit near its middle. It uses no DUT-internal names. Checks: (1) tx idles high after reset; (2) frame 1 start bit low; (3) frame 1 byte equals 0xA5 despite a mid-frame start pulse carrying 0x3C; (4) frame 1 stop bit high; (5) frame 2 byte equals 0x3C, sent normally from idle. Expected values come from the 8N1 clause and the 'start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored' clause. It is intended to fail check 3 on the RTL under investigation and pass all checks on the corrected RTL. It prints exactly one verdict line, guarded by a flag, and has a watchdog.", "entry": "uart_tx_rootcause_repro_tb"}], "tool_runs": ["run-0001", "run-0002"], "claims": ["The new repro uart_tx_rootcause_repro.v fails exactly check 3 (expected 0xA5, received 0x3D) on the RTL under investigation. This is predicted by hand-tracing the RTL; no run of it is cited.", "The same repro passes all 5 checks on a uart_tx that reloads hold only in IDLE. This is predicted, not run.", "The failed check in the regression and triage runs is the same reload-while-busy behaviour. The upstream testbench and regression log content were not visible to me.", "The nonce plusarg name is assumed. The testbench tries NIRMAAN_NONCE, nirmaan_nonce, NONCE and nonce, in that order."], "escalation": null, "notes": "run-0001 and run-0002 are triage-stage runs I relied on as evidence; I did not run them. The platform will run simulator.run with expect=fail over my testbench and the approved RTL. Risks for that gate: (a) the nonce plusarg name is not given in my inputs, so I try four common spellings; if none matches, the verdict line will carry the literal 'none' and the gate will not accept it; (b) the verdict uses %0s on a 128-byte reg, which relies on the simulator dropping leading NUL bytes. Timing assumes CLKS_PER_BIT=4 and a 10 ns clock, and the testbench sets that parameter explicitly. Sampling happens on negative clock edges, so it cannot race the transitions of tx at the positive edge. Independent review is still required; I do not approve my own work."}
